{
  "teamId": 143,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Philadelphia Phillies were a team that refused to let a slow start define them. They stumbled out of the gate with a .500 April and then cratered in May, going just 10-18 as their bullpen — particularly Jeurys Familia and Corey Knebel — repeatedly combusted in high-leverage moments. Familia surrendered a three-run homer to C.J. Cron in April, then a two-run shot to Justin Turner in May. Knebel was victimized for a crushing two-run double by Gavin Lux that swung nearly 79 points of win probability in a single blow. The team was flailing, and questions about whether this expensive roster could deliver were growing louder by the day.\n\nThen something shifted. Late May offered glimmers — a clutch Jean Segura single, a Bryce Harper two-run blast, a Nick Castellanos three-run shot — and those sparks lit a fire that blazed through June. Going 19-8 that month, the Phillies caught fire behind heroic contributions from unlikely sources. Bryson Stott crushed a go-ahead three-run homer on June 5th. Garrett Stubbs launched a stunning three-run shot of his own on June 15th worth over 82 points of win probability. Bryce Harper added a grand slam. The offense was alive, the rotation was holding, and Philadelphia was suddenly a dangerous team.\n\nThe summer months of July and August were a steady grind — 15-10 in July, 18-11 in August — marked by gritty wins even as the bullpen continued to provide occasional heartburn. David Robertson and Seranthony Domínguez each had damaging outings, and a Brad Hand-era triple gave away a game in late August. But the offense kept answering, with Bryson Stott emerging as a postseason-caliber contributor and the lineup's depth proving real. A September fade to 11-14 left the Phillies clinging to a wild card spot, but they had done enough over the long haul to earn their October ticket.\n\nThe postseason was the stuff of Philadelphia legend. Jean Segura's clutch single in the Wild Card round helped send the Cardinals home. Bryce Harper took over the NLCS against the Padres, launching a go-ahead two-run homer in Game 5 that has already become iconic. The Phillies reached the World Series for the first time since 2009, and they pushed the Houston Astros to six games. J.T. Realmuto's leadoff homer in the series was electric, and David Robertson gutted out a crucial hold in Game 3. But in the end, Yordan Alvarez delivered a brutal three-run walk-off blast off José Alvarado in Game 6, ending Philadelphia's improbable run just one win short of a championship. It was a season of resurrection, heartbreak, and genuine belief — a promise of more to come.",
